+        # "array must not contain infs or NaNs". psi reaching exactly 0 is worse:
+        # gigrnd raises ZeroDivisionError and the bare `except` above retries it
+        # forever, hanging the job rather than failing it.
+        #   1e-290 keeps 1.0/psi <= 1e290, i.e. 18 orders below float64 max, and is
+        # deliberately NOT PRScsx_mt's EFF_PSI_FLOOR = 1e-20. The two constants are
+        # not comparable: 1e-20 sits in the extreme tail of the MT product
+        # psi_pp*psi_t but in the bulk of psi here. Measured 2026-11-10 at n_snp=2000,
+        # n_iter=5000: with a 1e-20 clamp active ~2.1% of SNPs are clamped per
+        # iteration (MT's own floor: 0.73%), and unclamped 40-47% of psi values sit
+        # below 1e-20. On two boundary-check replicates it moved PRS-CSx corr_pred by
+        # -0.0019 overall and -0.0031 in EAS (95% CI [-0.0045, -0.0017], 27/32 cells
+        # negative, p = 3e-4) while leaving PRS-CSx-MT bit-identical -- i.e. it is a
+        # statistical intervention that handicaps the baseline in MT's favour, not a
+        # numerical guard.
+        #   At 1e-290 it is a guard and nothing else. Measured activity: 0 firings in
+        # the npop_p1 a=0.01 arms, and 13-19 firings over 5000 iterations (never more
+        # than one SNP at a time) at a=0.005 -- exactly the runaway events that
+        # otherwise kill the job. It never fires with two or more populations: min psi
+        # over 5000 iterations is 1.7e-199 at a=0.005 and 1.2e-195 to 3.0e-235 at
+        # a=0.002, 74 to 110 orders above the clamp. The 48 multi-population scenarios
+        # and the committed a-grid boundary check are therefore bit-identical with and
+        # without it. At a=1 psi never goes below 1.8e-09, so the untuned arm -- and
+        # comparability with the v2 sweep and PRSxtra -- is untouched.
+        psi[psi<1e-290] = 1e-290
 
